# Issues and the board: gh dojo

This project tracks the issues of acme/payments on the GitHub Projects board of acme, project 12, with the process in `.github/dojo.toml`. Take, move, comment on, block and create issues **only** through `gh dojo`: never change a status, a comment, a blocked-by link or an issue with `gh issue`, `gh project` or the API. For what gh dojo cannot do, such as editing an issue's title, body, labels or assignees, use `gh issue` only when the task needs it or the user asks.

Everything below is enough for everyday work; read `gh dojo <command> --help` only when a command refuses or you need a flag not named here. When the setup is broken (token, board, config), `gh dojo doctor` names each problem with the command that fixes it.

A bare `12` or `#12` is an issue of `acme/payments`; an issue of another repository is `owner/repo#12` or its URL.

## Lifecycle

`Backlog` → `In progress` → `In review` → `Approved` → `Merged`, through `start`, `review`, `approve`, `merged`.

- `Backlog` is the initial status: `gh dojo new` puts new issues there, and an issue without a status or not on the board counts as `Backlog`.
- Final: `Merged`. `gh dojo list` hides issues there unless `--all`.
- A blocker is resolved, no longer blocking, when it is closed or in `Merged`, though it may be open; one whose status could not be read (`status not read: …`) still blocks.

## Verbs

Run a verb as `gh dojo <verb> N`; `--dry-run` prints its plan without changing anything, and `--show` also prints the issue's card, as `gh dojo show N` does. A verb adds the issue to the board if it is missing and refuses a closed issue unless noted. It confirms in one line, such as `#42 Backlog → In progress, comment left`: there is no need to read the issue again.

`N…` takes several numbers at once: one that fails is skipped and named in the summary.

**`start`** (You begin writing the change): `gh dojo start N…`, Backlog → In progress; optional --comment; assigns you; comments "Started. Session: {session} {comment}".

- It is refused (exit code 2) while a blocker is unresolved: `gh dojo show N` lists them. Never move the status another way.

**`hold`** (Work is stuck: waiting for another issue or an outside decision): `gh dojo hold N…`, In progress, Changes requested → status unchanged; needs --by or --comment; labels +blocked; comments "On hold until {by}. {comment}".

- `--by 12` also marks the issue blocked by #12; text (`--by "the user's decision"`) only goes into the comment.

**`resume`** (What held the issue is resolved): `gh dojo resume N…`, In progress, Changes requested, Approved → status unchanged; labels -blocked; comments only with --comment.

- It is refused (exit code 2) while a blocker is unresolved: `gh dojo show N` lists them. Never move the status another way.

**`review`** (The pull request is open and ready for review): `gh dojo review N…`, In progress, Changes requested → In review; needs --comment and --assignee; refused with the label blocked; labels +needs-review -changes-requested; assigns the --assignee user; comments "Ready for review by @{assignee}: {comment}".

- It is refused (exit code 2) while the issue has the label `blocked`. `resume` removes `blocked`. Never remove a label another way to get it through; ask the user.
- It is refused (exit code 2) while a blocker is unresolved: `gh dojo show N` lists them. Never move the status another way.
- `--assignee <login>` (`@me` is you): ask the user whom to assign if you do not know.

**`request-changes`** (The reviewer asked for changes; the author makes them and runs review again): `gh dojo request-changes N…`, In review → Changes requested; needs --comment; labels +changes-requested -needs-review; assigns you; unassigns everyone else; comments "Changes requested: {comment}".

**`approve`** (The user approved the pull request after reviewing it): `gh dojo approve N…`, In review → Approved; optional --comment; refused with the label blocked; labels -needs-review; assigns you; unassigns everyone else; comments "Approved. {comment}".

- It is the user's decision: without `--yes` it exits with code 2. Ask the user, and run it again with `--yes` only if they agree; never add `--yes` on your own.
- It is refused (exit code 2) while the issue has the label `blocked`. `resume` removes `blocked`. Never remove a label another way to get it through; ask the user.

**`reject`** (The user decided the change is not wanted): `gh dojo reject N…`, In progress, In review, Changes requested, Approved → status unchanged; needs --comment; on a closed issue only if closed as not planned; labels -needs-review -changes-requested -blocked; comments "{comment}"; closes as not planned.

- It is the user's decision: without `--yes` it exits with code 2. Ask the user, and run it again with `--yes` only if they agree; never add `--yes` on your own.

**`merged`** (The approved pull request has been merged into main): `gh dojo merged N…`, Approved → Merged; also on closed issues; refused with the label blocked; comments only with --comment; closes as completed.

- It is refused (exit code 2) while the issue has the label `blocked`. `resume` removes `blocked`. Never remove a label another way to get it through; ask the user.
- For a release, `gh dojo release <tag> --verb merged --dry-run` lists the issues the release includes and its plan; read `gh dojo release --help` before you run it.

## Reading

- `gh dojo next`: what to take next, the open issues in `Backlog` or without a status, with no unresolved blocker, in board order, 5 by default (`--limit N`). The blocked ones are hidden and counted; `--all` shows them with their blockers. Filters: `--mine`, `--label <label>`. Once you pick one, `gh dojo start N --show`: it takes the issue and prints its card.
- `gh dojo show N` before you work on an issue (to take it as well, `gh dojo start N --show` prints the same card): its status, blockers both ways, parent and sub-issues, history, description and the last 5 comments; `--comments all` prints the whole thread, `--full` everything uncut (a verb takes both too). Several numbers print three lines per issue.
- `gh dojo list`: an overview of the board, when the user asks for one: the open issues grouped by status; closed issues and final statuses are hidden and counted. Filters: `--status "<status>"`, `--status none`, `--mine`, `--label <label>`, `--all`, `--limit 100`.
- `gh dojo next` and `gh dojo list` read the board a few seconds behind writes: an issue just created or moved may be missing or in its old status. To check one issue, `gh dojo show N`.

## Creating and linking issues

- `gh dojo new "<title>" --type <type> --body "…"`: the issue, its labels, the board in `Backlog`, in one command; a long body: `--body-file <file>`, or `--body-file -` for stdin. Also `--area <area>`, `--label <label>`, `--assignee @me`, `--milestone <title>`, `--blocked-by N`, `--parent N`, each checked before anything is created. `--type bug` adds the label `kind/bug`; the types are the repository's labels starting with `kind/`, and an unknown one fails listing them.
- `gh dojo comment N "text"`: a comment without changing the status.
- `gh dojo block N --by M` / `gh dojo unblock N --by M`: add or remove a blocked-by link without changing the status. An existing issue becomes a sub-issue with `gh issue edit N --parent M`.
- A commit pushed without a pull request names its issue after a keyword in its message, as `Fixes #12`; parentheses at the end of the first line, `(#12)`, do not count here: that is how `gh dojo release` finds the issues a release includes.

## Errors and repeats

On an error, do what its `fix:` line says. Exit code 2 is a process check refusing: never work around it (do not move the status another way); ask the user. On 4 (network, rate limit or auth) and 5 (partially applied), run the same command again, on 4 a bit later. A repeat is safe: what is already in place (a status, the latest comment, a link) is skipped, and `new` with the same title finishes the issue it created.
